1
00:00:04,618 --> 00:00:13,658
Record a payment and post it — Billing ▸ Payments · Payment Postings — Take the money, then post it against the claim so the balance is true.

2
00:00:19,022 --> 00:00:27,297
Billing ▸ Payments is money arriving — a card at the desk, a cheque in the post, an insurance transfer.

3
00:00:27,297 --> 00:00:41,220
Recording it here says the money exists. It does not yet say which claim it settles.

4
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:56,478
A patient co-payment: small, immediate, and tied to a visit rather than a remittance.

5
00:00:56,478 --> 00:01:03,790
Billing ▸ Payment Postings is the other half: attaching money to the claim it actually pays.

6
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:16,080
This is the step people skip. Skip it and the claim still reads unpaid, and your receivables report is wrong.

7
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:22,200
Choose the claim, and the payer and expected amount come with it.

8
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:31,242
Insurance money usually arrives as an electronic transfer with a remittance advice.

9
00:01:31,242 --> 00:01:43,444
The payer paid 144 of a 180 charge. The remaining 36 is not simply unpaid — it has to be explained.

10
00:01:43,444 --> 00:01:52,824
CO is a contractual obligation — the discount you agreed with this payer. You write it off; you never bill the patient for it.

11
00:01:52,824 --> 00:02:08,019
Code it PR instead and it becomes patient responsibility, and the patient gets an invoice. The code decides who pays.

12
00:02:08,019 --> 00:02:15,648
Posted. The claim balance clears, and receivables now reflect what the clinic is genuinely owed.

13
00:02:17,286 --> 00:02:33,706
What you just did

14
00:02:34,312 --> 00:02:43,312
Health | Efficiency | Growth — More in the Getting Started series — the next video is linked on screen.
